\section{Physical BRMM wave coordinate}
\label{sec:brmm-physical-wave-condition}

BRMM first specifies the \emph{physics} of the wave coordinate.  The vessel
translation is decomposed as
\[
 x_{\rm CoG}(t)=x_{\rm eq}(t)+p_m(t),\qquad
 v_m=\dot p_m,\qquad a_m=\dot v_m,
\]
where $p_m$ is wave-induced vessel displacement about a local equilibrium or
slow reference, not absolute global position. Current, propulsion, leeway,
secular translation, an arbitrary global-position origin, and other slow
reference motion belong to $x_{\rm eq}$ rather than to a permanent DC component
of $p_m$.  This decomposition does not remove physical acceleration from the
IMU: any nonzero $\ddot x_{\rm eq}$ must be explicitly compensated or retained
in the declared same-history model/disturbance budget.

\paragraph{Primary hard wave condition.}
A COMPLETE-BRMM wave history is a bounded oscillatory displacement history
about this local equilibrium.  In addition to the pointwise, window, bias,
attitude, and execution premises stated below, its same-history physical jet
must satisfy
\[
 \dot p_m=v_m,\qquad \dot v_m=a_m
\]
a.e., and the admitted physical family must possess a finite constant $D_S$
for which
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\int_u^t p_m(s)\,ds\right\|\le D_S
 \qquad\text{for every relevant }u,t
 \label{eq:brmm-primary-bounded-primitive}
\end{equation}
uniformly over every successive admitted continuation.  Equation
\eqref{eq:brmm-primary-bounded-primitive} is the pathwise mathematical content
of ``bounded oscillatory wave displacement'' needed by the OU--III theorem.
It rules out a permanent nonzero displacement offset being integrated forever
as wave motion, while still allowing quiet water and finite flat segments.

The bound $D_S$ is a property of the physical wave family.  It is not the old
$300\,\mathrm{m\,s}$ P4 working radius, is not selected because a stability
certificate happens to pass, and is not implied by asymptotic zero mean, a
power spectrum, significant wave height, a finite replay, or independent
pointwise bounds on $p_m,v_m,a_m$.  A zero-mean statement alone is insufficient
because it does not prevent arbitrarily long deterministic excursions.

At the actual Live time $t_L$, the one-time centered coordinate is
\[
 S_L(t)=\int_{t_L}^{t}p_m(s)\,ds,
 \qquad S_L(t_L)=0,
\]
and the primary physical condition gives $\|S_L(t)\|\le D_S$.  This is a
single change of proof origin.  Position is not re-anchored and $S_L$ is not
re-zeroed at later proof words or hybrid transitions.

\paragraph{How the physical condition may be certified.}
Harmonic/spectral and shaping-state models are not the definition of BRMM
wave motion.  They are sufficient mathematical constructions for proving that
a concrete physical wave family satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-primary-bounded-primitive}
and for computing a hard $D_S$.

For example, a positive-frequency hard amplitude representation
\[
 p_m(t)=\int\cos(\omega t)\,dA(\omega)
       +\int\sin(\omega t)\,dB(\omega)
\]
with finite inverse-frequency amplitude moment
\[
 M_{-1}=\int\omega^{-1}\bigl(d|A|+d|B|\bigr)<\infty
\]
has the bounded primitive
\[
 \phi_w(t)=\int\frac{\sin(\omega t)\,dA(\omega)
                         -\cos(\omega t)\,dB(\omega)}{\omega},
\qquad \dot\phi_w=p_m,
\]
and therefore $D_S\le2M_{-1}$.  A separately qualified hard cutoff
$\omega\ge\omega_{\min}>0$ and amplitude mass $M_0$ imply
$D_S\le2M_0/\omega_{\min}$.  These are deterministic amplitude bounds, not
PSD or Gaussian high-probability arguments.

Likewise, a bounded shaping-state representation can certify the same physical
condition when it proves an exact potential identity $\dot\phi_w=p_m$ and a
uniform hard bound on $\phi_w$.  Such a representation is one proof method;
it is not a requirement that every physically admissible sea be described by
that particular realization.  Any other deterministic representation may be
used if it proves the same all-time bounded-wave condition and preserves the
same-history $p_m,v_m,a_m$ and centered-primitive ancestry.

The executable proof tooling currently implements the spectral/amplitude and
shaping-state certificate routes.  Accordingly, later phrases such as
``generator certificate'', ``generator condition'', or ``generator state'' in
implementation-oriented proof text refer only to the internal representation
used by one of those certificate routes.  They do \emph{not} redefine the
physical COMPLETE-BRMM admissibility condition stated in this section.

The historical PR~\#515 witness $p_m(t)=d\ne0$, $v_m=a_m=0$ was admitted only
because the former finite-window source omitted this physical condition.  It
satisfies local displacement and short-window increment caps, but
$\|\int_u^t p_m\,ds\|=(t-u)\|d\|$ violates every finite $D_S$ for sufficiently
large $t-u$.  Thus it remains a regression witness for the old definition,
classified as obstruction B there and as source-specification omission E
relative to the intended physics.  It is not evidence that the nominal
shipping filter is unstable.
